{"payload":{"candidate":{"prompt":"You are given a JSON input containing two parts: a system_profile describing the software installed on a system (product names and versions), and an advisories array where each advisory has an ID, a description, and affected-product information (affected versions/ranges, and fixed or excluded versions).\n\nYour job: identify every advisory from the supplied list that applies to the installed system, and return ONLY a JSON object of the form {\"applicable_cves\":[\"CVE-...\"]} containing unique advisory IDs taken verbatim from the supplied list. If none apply, return {\"applicable_cves\":[]}. Do not invent, transform, or truncate IDs. Output only that JSON object — no explanations, no markdown, no extra keys.\n\nMatching rules:\n\n1. PRODUCT NAME MATCHING. Advisory affected-product names may differ stylistically from the installed product names. Treat the following as equivalent when comparing names: package-manager style names (e.g., 'libxml2-dev'), vendor-stripped names (vendor/product prefixes removed), names using underscores instead of spaces, and Title Case names. Normalize both sides (lowercase, strip vendor prefixes, replace underscores with hyphens or spaces, drop common package suffixes like '-dev', '-lib', 'lib' prefixes where the core product name matches) before comparing. Only match when the advisory is actually about the installed product; never report advisories for products not installed.\n\n2. VERSION COMPARISON. Compare the installed version against the advisory's affected version information. Handle versions with 1, 2, 3, or 4+ segments by zero-padding (e.g., treat 1.2 as 1.2.0.0 when comparing against 1.2.0.3). Compare segment-by-segment numerically.\n\n3. BOUNDS. Advisories may state their version bounds either as structured data (fields like affected_versions, version ranges, 'less than', 'fixed in') or in prose within the description (e.g., 'versions prior to 2.4.1', 'affects 3.x through 3.9.2', 'fixed in 1.0.5'). Parse BOTH forms carefully:\n   - If the installed version falls within the affected range (inclusive of stated endpoints unless explicitly exclusive), the advisory APPLIES.\n   - If the advisory states a fixed version, patched version, or an exclusion (e.g., 'not affected: 2.1.3 and later', 'does not affect 4.0'), and the installed version is at or above that bound, the advisory does NOT apply — do not report it.\n   - If the installed version is BELOW a stated minimum affected version, the advisory does not apply.\n   - Do not assume 'newer is affected' or 'older is affected'; use only the bounds actually given in the advisory, whether rendered in structured fields or in prose.\n\n4. COMMON PITFALLS.\n   - An advisory is applicable even when the installed version number appears below or above the numbers prominently mentioned in the description, IF the stated affected range or excluded range includes it. Read the full range carefully; do not exclude an advisory merely because one endpoint differs from the installed version.\n   - Conversely, do not include an advisory merely because the product name matches; verify the version is not excluded by a fixed/patched bound.\n   - Version segments: 1.2.0.3 vs 1.2.0 are DIFFERENT versions; compare numerically with padding, and respect the advisory's own bounds.\n\n5. OUTPUT. Collect the IDs of all advisories that pass both the product-name check and the version check. Deduplicate. Return exactly {\"applicable_cves\":[...]} as valid JSON, with IDs copied exactly as supplied (e.g., 'CVE-2023-1234'). Never output any other text."},"handle":"fe9c0a0d7d5d202d327c0d121113e432e37f06736db30c2f20cdd58e8503b6b0","profile_ids":["optimization_0201","optimization_0096","optimization_0169","optimization_0034","optimization_0143","optimization_0038","optimization_0087","optimization_0192","optimization_0109","optimization_0214","optimization_0071","optimization_0068","optimization_0117","optimization_0024","optimization_0007","opt